(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["pharmacology-i-exam-3-pharm-e3-drill-diuretics-heart-failure"]={"title":"Diuretics & Heart Failure Drugs Drill","category":"Pharmacology I","exam":"Exam 3","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which diuretic class inhibits the sodium-potassium-chloride carrier in the thick ascending limb of the loop of Henle?","choices":["Loop diuretics","Thiazide diuretics","Potassium-sparing diuretics","Carbonic anhydrase inhibitors"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Loop diuretics inhibit the Na-2Cl-K carrier on the luminal membrane of the thick ascending limb. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Which diuretic class inhibits the sodium-chloride transporter in the luminal membrane of the distal convoluted tubule?","choices":["Loop diuretics","Thiazide diuretics","Carbonic anhydrase inhibitors","Aldosterone antagonists"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Thiazides inhibit the Na/Cl transporter in the luminal membrane of the distal convoluted tubule, their major action. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Which diuretic class works in the proximal tubule by blocking bicarbonate reabsorption?","choices":["Loop diuretics","Thiazide diuretics","Carbonic anhydrase inhibitors","Aldosterone antagonists"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Carbonic anhydrase inhibitors decrease bicarbonate absorption in the proximal tubule by 80 to 90 percent. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"Which diuretic class is the most potent, increasing urine output by up to 4 liters per day?","choices":["Thiazide diuretics","Potassium-sparing diuretics","Carbonic anhydrase inhibitors","Loop diuretics"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Loop diuretics inhibit sodium chloride reabsorption by 20 to 25 percent and can raise urine output up to 4 liters per day. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Which diuretic class is the mainstay of heart failure therapy for relieving fluid overload?","choices":["Loop diuretics","Thiazide diuretics","Potassium-sparing diuretics","Carbonic anhydrase inhibitors"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Loop diuretics are the mainstay of heart failure therapy; they decrease sodium and water retention and so lower preload. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"Which diuretic class decreases renal calcium excretion and can raise serum calcium slightly?","choices":["Loop diuretics","Thiazide diuretics","Carbonic anhydrase inhibitors","Potassium-sparing diuretics"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Thiazides decrease renal calcium excretion by increasing calcium reabsorption, so serum calcium can rise. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"Which diuretic class is most often used in combination with other diuretics or antihypertensive drugs because it is much less natriuretic?","choices":["Loop diuretics","Thiazide diuretics","Potassium-sparing diuretics","Carbonic anhydrase inhibitors"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Potassium-sparing diuretics have much less natriuretic and diuretic effect, and are most often used in combination. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"Which diuretic class works best in elderly and African American patients with hypertension and sodium-retentive states?","choices":["Loop diuretics","Carbonic anhydrase inhibitors","Potassium-sparing diuretics","Thiazide diuretics"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Thiazides work best in elderly patients, African Americans and sodium-retentive states, and low doses are preferred for hypertension. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"Which diuretic class can cause hyperkalemia, especially when combined with angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitors or potassium supplements?","choices":["Potassium-sparing diuretics","Loop diuretics","Thiazide diuretics","Carbonic anhydrase inhibitors"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Hyperkalemia is the key adverse effect of potassium-sparing diuretics, with caution advised with angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitors and potassium supplements. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"Which diuretic class stays effective in patients with a creatinine clearance below 30 mL/min?","choices":["Thiazide diuretics","Loop diuretics","Potassium-sparing diuretics","Carbonic anhydrase inhibitors"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Loop diuretics remain effective when creatinine clearance is below 30 mL/min. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"Which diuretic class can be added to saline hydration to help treat hypercalcemia?","choices":["Thiazide diuretics","Carbonic anhydrase inhibitors","Loop diuretics","Aldosterone antagonists"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Saline hydration is the main treatment, and loop diuretics can be added alongside it because they increase calcium excretion. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"Which diuretic class is used for primary aldosteronism?","choices":["Loop diuretics","Thiazide diuretics","Carbonic anhydrase inhibitors","Aldosterone antagonists"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Primary aldosteronism is the first listed clinical use of aldosterone antagonists; they work best when aldosterone is high. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"Which of these is a loop diuretic?","choices":["Torsemide","Indapamide","Triamterene","Dichlorphenamide"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Furosemide, bumetanide, torsemide and ethacrynic acid are the loop diuretics; torsemide inhibits the sodium-potassium-chloride carrier of the thick ascending limb. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Which drug can cause ototoxicity by damaging the cochlea, the hearing organ of the inner ear?","choices":["Hydrochlorothiazide","Furosemide","Amiloride","Spironolactone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Ototoxicity, damage to the cochlea (classically described as hair-cell damage, though often reversible), is a listed adverse effect of loop diuretics such as furosemide. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Which drug increases the excretion of potassium, calcium and magnesium?","choices":["Hydrochlorothiazide","Amiloride","Furosemide","Eplerenone"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Loop diuretics increase potassium excretion and calcium and magnesium excretion. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Which drug's clearance is decreased by loop diuretics, raising its toxicity?","choices":["Gentamicin","Warfarin","Ibuprofen","Lithium"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Loop diuretics decrease lithium clearance and increase its toxicity. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"Which drug blunts the natriuretic and blood pressure response to loop diuretics by blocking prostaglandins?","choices":["Ibuprofen","Lithium","Gentamicin","Warfarin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs such as ibuprofen blunt the natriuretic and blood pressure response of loop diuretics. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"Which drug has its ototoxicity potentiated by loop diuretics?","choices":["Ibuprofen","Gentamicin","Lithium","Warfarin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Aminoglycosides such as gentamicin have their ototoxicity potentiated by loop diuretics. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"Which drug competes with loop diuretics for plasma protein binding?","choices":["Gentamicin","Lithium","Warfarin","Ibuprofen"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Warfarin competes with loop diuretics for plasma protein binding. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"Which thiazide-type drug is classically singled out as effective at a low creatinine clearance?","choices":["Hydrochlorothiazide","Chlorthalidone","Indapamide","Metolazone"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Metolazone is the thiazide-type drug singled out as effective at lower creatinine clearance rates, unlike the other thiazides. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"Which drug lowers urinary calcium and is used to prevent calcium oxalate kidney stones?","choices":["Hydrochlorothiazide","Furosemide","Bumetanide","Acetazolamide"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Thiazides decrease renal calcium excretion, and calcium oxalate stones are a listed indication. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"Which drug can cause hypercalcemia as an adverse effect?","choices":["Furosemide","Hydrochlorothiazide","Torsemide","Ethacrynic acid"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Hypercalcemia is a listed adverse effect of thiazides, because they decrease renal calcium excretion. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"Which drug works by binding the aldosterone receptor and blocking it?","choices":["Amiloride","Triamterene","Spironolactone","Acetazolamide"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Spironolactone binds the steroid receptor but does not translocate to the nucleus, blocking aldosterone's actions. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"Which potassium-sparing diuretic is linked to megaloblastic anemia?","choices":["Amiloride","Spironolactone","Eplerenone","Triamterene"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Megaloblastic anemia is the distinctive adverse effect of triamterene. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"Which potassium-sparing diuretic is linked to azotemia?","choices":["Amiloride","Triamterene","Spironolactone","Eplerenone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Azotemia is the distinctive listed adverse effect of amiloride among the potassium-sparing diuretics. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"Which potassium-sparing diuretic is marketed in combination with hydrochlorothiazide as Moduretic?","choices":["Triamterene","Amiloride","Spironolactone","Eplerenone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Amiloride (Midamor) is combined with hydrochlorothiazide as Moduretic. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"Which drug causes gynecomastia and testicular atrophy in men?","choices":["Eplerenone","Amiloride","Spironolactone","Triamterene"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Spironolactone also binds androgen receptors and blocks them, which causes gynecomastia and testicular atrophy in men. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"Which drug causes menstrual irregularities in women through its effect on androgen receptors?","choices":["Eplerenone","Amiloride","Triamterene","Spironolactone"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Spironolactone blocks androgen receptors as well as aldosterone receptors, which causes menstrual irregularities in women. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"Which aldosterone antagonist is the alternative when spironolactone causes gynecomastia?","choices":["Eplerenone","Amiloride","Triamterene","Acetazolamide"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Eplerenone has less effect on androgen receptors and rarely causes gynecomastia, so it replaces spironolactone when that develops. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 73"},{"q":"Which heart failure drug is not given when the serum potassium is above 5 or the serum creatinine is above 2.5?","choices":["Carvedilol","Spironolactone","Furosemide","Ivabradine"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Patients are not eligible for spironolactone if potassium is above 5 or serum creatinine is above 2.5, because of hyperkalemia risk. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 73"},{"q":"Which diuretic is used to prevent mountain sickness?","choices":["Furosemide","Spironolactone","Acetazolamide","Hydrochlorothiazide"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Mountain sickness is a listed use of carbonic anhydrase inhibitors such as acetazolamide. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"Which diuretic is also used for epilepsy, through metabolic acidosis and central nervous system effects?","choices":["Furosemide","Hydrochlorothiazide","Spironolactone","Acetazolamide"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Epilepsy is a listed other use of carbonic anhydrase inhibitors, through metabolic acidosis and central nervous system effects. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"Which drug causes metabolic acidosis, potassium depletion and drowsiness?","choices":["Acetazolamide","Furosemide","Hydrochlorothiazide","Bumetanide"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Metabolic acidosis, potassium depletion and drowsiness are the listed adverse effects of carbonic anhydrase inhibitors. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"Which of these is a carbonic anhydrase inhibitor?","choices":["Metolazone","Methazolamide","Amiloride","Eplerenone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Acetazolamide, dichlorphenamide and methazolamide are the carbonic anhydrase inhibitors listed. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"Which of these is a carbonic anhydrase inhibitor?","choices":["Chlorthalidone","Chlorothiazide","Dichlorphenamide","Torsemide"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Dichlorphenamide is one of the three carbonic anhydrase inhibitors listed, with acetazolamide and methazolamide. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"Which drug class lowers preload and afterload and decreases left ventricular remodeling in heart failure?","choices":["Digitalis glycosides","Loop diuretics","Phosphodiesterase inhibitors","Angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitors"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. These inhibitors decrease preload, afterload and sympathetic activation, and decrease hypertrophy, dilation and remodeling. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"Which drug class causes a dry cough and angioedema in patients with heart failure?","choices":["Angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitors","Angiotensin receptor blockers","Loop diuretics","Beta blockers"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Cough and angioedema are listed problems of these inhibitors, along with renal impairment, hypotension and raised potassium. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"Which drug class raises serum potassium and can impair renal function and cause hypotension in heart failure?","choices":["Loop diuretics","Angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitors","Thiazide diuretics","Beta blockers"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Impaired renal function, hypotension and raised serum potassium are listed problems of these inhibitors. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"Which drug class is first-line therapy in heart failure of classes II to IV along with the angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitors?","choices":["Digitalis glycosides","Loop diuretics","Beta blockers","Thiazide diuretics"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Beta blockers are first-line therapy in heart failure of classes II to IV, and patients should be on both regardless of symptoms. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 60"},{"q":"Which drug class could decompensate a heart failure patient if started too fast and so is begun low and slowly?","choices":["Angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitors","Aldosterone antagonists","Loop diuretics","Beta blockers"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Beta blockers are started at very low doses and titrated up slowly, with the patient stable and monitored for worsening heart failure. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"Which of these beta blockers is one of the three that reduce mortality in heart failure?","choices":["Carvedilol","Atenolol","Propranolol","Nadolol"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Carvedilol, metoprolol succinate and bisoprolol are the beta blockers with a mortality benefit in heart failure. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"Which long-acting beta blocker formulation reduces mortality in heart failure?","choices":["Atenolol","Metoprolol succinate","Propranolol","Labetalol"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Metoprolol succinate (the extended-release form) is one of the three beta blockers with a mortality benefit in heart failure. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"Which beta blocker, besides carvedilol and metoprolol succinate, reduces mortality in heart failure?","choices":["Atenolol","Nadolol","Bisoprolol","Propranolol"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Bisoprolol is the third beta blocker with a mortality benefit in heart failure. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"Which drug has a lactone ring and a steroid nucleus that are essential for its activity?","choices":["Milrinone","Inamrinone","Ivabradine","Digoxin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Digoxin's lactone ring and steroid nucleus are essential for activity, and its sugar molecules influence absorption, half-life and metabolism. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"Which drug inhibits the sodium-potassium ATPase in cardiac muscle, raising intracellular calcium?","choices":["Digoxin","Milrinone","Dobutamine","Ivabradine"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Digitalis inhibits Na+/K+ ATPase, raising intracellular sodium and then calcium through the sodium-calcium exchanger, so contraction strengthens. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"Which drug raises parasympathetic activity at the atrioventricular node and lowers sympathetic activity as part of its neurohormonal action?","choices":["Dobutamine","Digoxin","Milrinone","Dopamine"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Digoxin increases parasympathetic activity in the atrioventricular node and conduction system and decreases sympathetic activity. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"Which heart failure drug causes xanthopsia, seeing yellow-green halos around lights?","choices":["Milrinone","Dobutamine","Digoxin","Carvedilol"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Visual disturbances such as blurred vision and yellow-green halos (xanthopsia) are signs of digoxin toxicity. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"Which heart failure drug improves symptoms and exercise tolerance and reduces hospitalizations but gives no survival benefit?","choices":["Carvedilol","Lisinopril","Spironolactone","Digoxin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Digoxin's clinical benefits are symptoms, exercise tolerance, quality of life and fewer hospitalizations, with no survival benefit. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"Which drug can be used for rate control in patients with atrial fibrillation and heart failure?","choices":["Digoxin","Milrinone","Dobutamine","Sacubitril-valsartan"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Digoxin has rate-control properties and can be used in patients who have atrial fibrillation with heart failure. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"Which drug is contraindicated in advanced atrioventricular block and in Wolff-Parkinson-White syndrome?","choices":["Furosemide","Digoxin","Lisinopril","Spironolactone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Advanced atrioventricular block, severe bradycardia and Wolff-Parkinson-White syndrome are listed contraindications to digoxin. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 71"},{"q":"Which drug lengthens the PR interval, shortens the QT interval and depresses the ST segment?","choices":["Milrinone","Dobutamine","Digoxin","Dopamine"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Digoxin toxicity shows nodal slowing, a longer PR interval, a shorter QT interval and ST depression, with bradycardia. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 70"},{"q":"Which agent, made by immunizing sheep, rapidly reverses digoxin toxicity?","choices":["Milrinone","Furosemide","Spironolactone","Digoxin immune Fab"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Digoxin immune Fab binds digoxin with higher affinity than the sodium-potassium pump does, rapidly reversing toxicity. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"Which drug is an inotrope that works by inhibiting phosphodiesterase III and approved for short-term intravenous use?","choices":["Milrinone","Digoxin","Ivabradine","Carvedilol"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Milrinone is a cyclic adenosine monophosphate phosphodiesterase (III) inhibitor, approved for short-term intravenous use in acute decompensated heart failure. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 74"},{"q":"Which phosphodiesterase III inhibitor is more likely to cause thrombocytopenia?","choices":["Milrinone","Inamrinone","Dobutamine","Dopamine"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Thrombocytopenia is listed for these inotropes, with milrinone causing less than inamrinone. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 76"},{"q":"Which inotrope is a selective beta-1 agonist that stimulates the force of contraction more than the rate?","choices":["Dopamine","Milrinone","Dobutamine","Inamrinone"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Dobutamine is a selective beta-1 agonist given by intravenous infusion for short-term stabilization. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 77"},{"q":"Which inotrope acts through both dopamine and beta receptors?","choices":["Dobutamine","Milrinone","Inamrinone","Dopamine"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Dopamine infusions act through both dopamine receptors and beta receptors. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 77"},{"q":"Which drug blocks the hyperpolarization-activated cyclic nucleotide-gated channel in the sinoatrial node?","choices":["Ivabradine","Carvedilol","Digoxin","Milrinone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Ivabradine blocks this channel, inhibiting the pacemaker current in the sinoatrial node and reducing heart rate. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 79"},{"q":"Which drug lowers heart rate without affecting contractility?","choices":["Carvedilol","Ivabradine","Metoprolol succinate","Bisoprolol"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Ivabradine reduces heart rate and does not affect contractility. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 79"},{"q":"Which drug is added in sinus rhythm with a heart rate above 70 when a patient is maxed out on beta blockers?","choices":["Dobutamine","Milrinone","Ivabradine","Dopamine"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Ivabradine is used in patients maxed out on beta blockers who are in normal sinus rhythm with a heart rate above 70. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 79"},{"q":"Which drug causes phosphenes, transient brightness in a limited area of the visual field?","choices":["Digoxin","Milrinone","Sacubitril-valsartan","Ivabradine"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Ivabradine affects retinal photoreceptors and causes transient brightness, halos and multiple images, called phosphenes. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 80"},{"q":"Which drug inhibits neprilysin, the enzyme that degrades natriuretic peptide and bradykinin?","choices":["Sacubitril","Valsartan","Lisinopril","Spironolactone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Sacubitril is a neprilysin inhibitor, formulated with valsartan, so natriuretic peptide and bradykinin build up and cause vasodilation. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 81"},{"q":"Which drug must not be given with an angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitor and needs a 36-hour washout when switching?","choices":["Ivabradine","Sacubitril-valsartan","Dapagliflozin","Carvedilol"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Sacubitril-valsartan is not to be used with an angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitor, and a 36-hour washout avoids adverse effects. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 81"},{"q":"Which drug blocks the sodium-glucose transporter in the proximal tubule and reduces heart failure deaths and hospitalizations?","choices":["Acetazolamide","Spironolactone","Dapagliflozin","Milrinone"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Dapagliflozin, a sodium-glucose cotransporter 2 inhibitor, stops the kidneys reabsorbing glucose and reduces mortality and hospitalizations in chronic heart failure. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 82"},{"q":"Which drug carries a risk of fungal urinary tract infections because it increases sugar in the urine?","choices":["Eplerenone","Ivabradine","Sacubitril-valsartan","Empagliflozin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Sodium-glucose cotransporter 2 inhibitors such as empagliflozin cause glucose loss in the urine and risk hypotension and fungal infections. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 82"},{"q":"Which heart failure drug causes fatigue, confusion, dizziness and abnormal dreams when its level is too high?","choices":["Digoxin","Milrinone","Dobutamine","Ivabradine"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Central symptoms of digoxin toxicity include delirium, fatigue, confusion, dizziness and abnormal dreams. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 69"},{"q":"Which drug reduces mortality in advanced (class III or IV) heart failure through neurohormonal inhibition and slowed remodeling?","choices":["Digoxin","Spironolactone","Furosemide","Milrinone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Spironolactone reduces mortality in grade III or IV heart failure, with neurohormonal inhibition and slowed remodeling of the left ventricle. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 73"},{"q":"Which drug is applied to the eye as drops to lower bicarbonate in the ciliary body in glaucoma?","choices":["Furosemide","Spironolactone","Dorzolamide","Hydrochlorothiazide"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Dorzolamide lowers bicarbonate in the ciliary body and is used for glaucoma, one of the other uses of carbonic anhydrase inhibition. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 42"}]};
